# 11. Constraints

## Purpose

Project constraints define the boundaries, limitations, and conditions within which the Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture (AVIA) project shall be planned, developed, validated, and maintained.

Constraints are recognized as essential engineering inputs that guide objective decision-making and promote focused innovation.

---

## Project Constraints

### PC-001 — Scientific Constraints

The project shall be developed in accordance with established scientific principles and validated scientific knowledge.

---

### PC-002 — Physical Constraints

All proposed engineering concepts shall remain consistent with the laws of physics and the physical behavior of real-world vehicle systems.

---

### PC-003 — Engineering Constraints

Engineering activities shall follow approved systems engineering practices, project governance, and engineering philosophy.

---

### PC-004 — Resource Constraints

Project progress shall consider the availability of time, knowledge, tools, computational resources, funding, and engineering personnel.

---

### PC-005 — Technological Constraints

Engineering solutions shall acknowledge the capabilities and limitations of available technologies while remaining adaptable to future technological advancements.

---

### PC-006 — Safety Constraints

Safety shall remain a fundamental consideration throughout engineering analysis, architectural development, validation, and future implementation.

---

### PC-007 — Documentation Constraints

Engineering decisions, assumptions, and changes shall be documented and maintained within the governed repository to preserve traceability and knowledge integrity.

---

## Engineering Perspective

Within the AVIA project, constraints are not regarded as obstacles to progress. They define the engineering space within which disciplined analysis, creative problem-solving, and practical innovation occur.

The project adopts the principle that **constraints guide innovation** by focusing engineering effort toward solutions that are scientifically sound, technically feasible, and aligned with project objectives.
